\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Conceptual structure of the LLM-guided code-evolution loop studied in this dissertation. The research question is not whether a single prompt can yield a useful heuristic, but whether the closed loop between archive, validator, and proposal model produces reliable adaptation under controlled protocols.}}{6}{figure.1.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Representative held-out results from completed project phases. Lower is better in both panels. In the TSP replay suite, random replay achieved the best mean transfer gap among the replay variants. In the real-world CVRP suite, solver evolution improved over weaker bounded baselines yet remained behind the fixed Clarke--Wright savings heuristic on the primary endpoint.}}{9}{figure.1.2}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Conceptual synthesis of the research threads integrated by the dissertation. The gap is not any single ingredient in isolation, but the absence of a cross-family, validator-aware account of how these ingredients interact when the object of search is executable heuristic code.}}{19}{figure.2.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Progression of the completed experimental program. The framework begins with low-cost behavioral adaptation in games, then strengthens the claims through benchmark routing, mechanism studies, higher-level search objects, and bounded real-world CVRP.}}{21}{figure.3.1}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Family-level held-out outcomes for the simple-game transfer suite. Territory control shows the strongest positive transfer, pursuit/evasion remains moderately positive, and resource-collection/denial is the weakest environment.}}{28}{figure.4.1}%
